          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does low iron make people tired?",
          "answer": "Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\nThe sources I searched do not explain why low iron causes tiredness. They list fatigue as a common symptom but do not describe the mechanism.\n\nThe \"Iron-deficiency anemia\" reference (General clinical reference, educational summary) says fatigue is one of the usual signs, along with reduced exercise tolerance, breathlessness on exertion, palpitations, headache, dizziness, pale skin, cold hands and feet, brittle nails, and hair thinning. It also says the onset is usually gradual, and that the body compensates well, so people often blame months of growing tiredness on other things.\n\nThe results did not cover the biology behind the link. If you want to understand it, ask a clinician or pharmacist, or look for a reference that covers iron's role in the body.\n\nIf you are feeling tired and wondering whether iron could be part of it, a clinician can check with a blood test. The same reference notes that ferritin, the usual test of iron stores, can look normal during inflammation, so further iron tests may be needed.",
